WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.460
With all the recent political nonsense that's been going on

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.200
People are throwing the word "orwellian" around more than usual

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.460
Whether or not those concerns are founded

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:15.880
There's been a lot of speculation regarding just what kind of horrible future the world is headed towards

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.800
Will we really live in a dystopian society like George Orwell presented in his novel in 1984

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.960
In my opinion, probably not

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.760
But where's the fun in that?

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:28.360
With all the talk of Russian medelling in the recent U.S. Presidential Election

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.400
Tension between nuclear powers is brewing

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.800
Now the world has avoided nuclear war until now, but what if that were to change?

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.820
How many nuclear bombs would it take to eradicate humanity?

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.640
The last time Nuclear weapons were used in war was in 1945, on the Japanese cities of Hiroshima &amp; Nagasaki, at the end of WWII

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.480
These bombs, dubbed Fat Man and Little Boy

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.180
Would be considered primitive by today's standards, and were actually two different types of bombs

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.160
Little Boy, the explosive that destroyed Hiroshima

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.900
Was a uranium gun-type bomb which was detonated by firing one piece of sub-critical material into another

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.880
Triggering the nuclear reaction

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.440
Fat Man was  a plutonium implosion-type bomb

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.580
Which was triggered by essentially using smaller explosives to squeeze the bomb's plutonium core

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:13.220
In terms of explosive power, Little Boy is estimated to have been between 12 and 18 kilotonnes of TNT

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.100
And Fat Man between 18 and 23 kilotonnes

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.740
A kiloton is the measurement of the explosive power of one-thousand tons of TNT

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.240
By comparison, a modern nuclear warhead can have a yield exceeding one megaton

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.640
And modern hydrogen bombs which I've lumped into the same catogory for this video

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:34.300
Can exceed 3.8 megatons which is 260 times more powerful than the explosive that destroyed Hiroshima

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:41.680
Even with their comparatively low yield, Little Boy and Fat Man wiped out between 113,000 and 226,000 people between them

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.340
With roughly half of those deaths occurring on the first day

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.720
With later deaths being caused by both burn, radiation sickness, and other injuries

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:51.640
If that kind of destructive power was packed into just 2 primitive nukes

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.880
What kind of scenario are we looking at today?

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:59.140
For this thought experiment, we're going to consider the standard US B83 nuclear bomb our baseline

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.260
With an explosive yield of 1.2 megatons

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.960
We're also going to give Japan a break and use my hometown of Dallas, Texas for ground zero to start the end of the world.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.900
So, without further ado, lets blow up my neighborhood.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:15.560
The estimated fatalities from dropping a 1.2 megaton nuke smack-dab in the middle of Dallas are estimated to be around

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.860
267,000 with another 649,000 injured

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.540
The initial fireball would have a radius of over a kilometer

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.420
Anything within that radius would be instantly vaporized

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.400
Moving further away from ground zero,

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.420
The air blast radius would be roughly 7 and a half kilometers

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.780
Within this range, most residential buildings would be obliterated with considerable fatalities

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.920
Even further out at the thermal radiation radius of 13.2 kilometers is where survival becomes slightly more possible

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:46.120
If you did manage to escape the worst of the blast, you'd still be covered head to toe in 3rd degree burns

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:52.420
But don't worry, you wouldn't feel it because these burns would extend through your layers of skin and destroy the nerves responsible for registering pain

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:57.140
Okay, so if we're looking at 300,000 dead on impact from one B83 bomb

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.980
Without taking radiation poisoning and other injuries into account

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.780
How many bombs would it take to wipe out the entire population of the massive Dallas-Fort Worth metroplex?

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:09.620
As of 2016, DFW had a population of about 7.1 million people

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:15.960
Assuming each bomb killed 300,000 people, it would take 24 bombs to eradicate every single person at once

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.360
Of course, for whoever is nuking Dallas, that would be hugely wasteful

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.500
If the bad guys wanted to kill every human on the planet at once it would take over a million bombs

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.500
Which vastly exceeds the roughly 15,000 available

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.020
And even then, many of those aren't as powerful as B83s

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.980
So they'd have to take a different approach

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:38.120
Instead of simply aiming for the fatalities at ground zero, the right tactic would be to take out any infrastructure and agriculture

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.960
A recent scientific report suggests that 100 nuclear detonations in a fairly short period of time

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.680
Would produce 5 teragrams, or 5 billion kilograms of soot

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:49.180
That would clog the Earth's stratosphere and block sunlight

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.360
Destroying much of the planet's ozone layer and triggering a drop in global temperature

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.560
That could last over 25 years

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.600
This temperature drop combined with the roughly 80% increase in ultraviolet radiation from the wrecked ozone layer

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.540
Would destroy many land and sea based ecosystems and spark a global famine

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.020
To kill 2 birds with one nuclear stone

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:09.320
If we were to target 100 cultural hubs and industrial areas at once

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:14.220
That would knock out electricity, transportation, running water, food production, communication

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.780
And would cause the long term global effects we just looked at

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.420
To make extra sure that the lethal effects of nuclear war are fully realized without going overboard

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.160
We can consider the average spread of radiation from our B83 bombs

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:30.480
Over the course of a week, a lethal dose of radiation can creep up to 144km from ground zero

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:35.960
Giving us an effective lethal range of about 65,869 square kilometers per bomb

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:39.360
So, if we take the total amount of land inhabited by humans

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:45.540
Roughly 18.6 million square kilometers and divide that by our lethal range, you're looking at 283 bombs

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.700
If everything went according to our evil plan

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.780
And we took out all the big cultural hubs around the world

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.840
That would mean that functional hospitals would be few and far between

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.480
And would be unable to treat the victims of severe burns and radiation poisoning

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.380
Over the course of the two weeks following the attack

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:03.260
Millions, if not billions, would die from their injuries and severe radiation poisoning

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:10.740
So, hypothetically speaking, it could feasibly take as few as 100 B83 bombs to knock humanity into critically endangered status

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.260
To be thorough and basically ensure extinction would take around 300

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:18.160
Could isolated pockets of humanity survive this nuclear winter?

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:23.460
Possibly. If they managed to avoid the radiation at some remote Siberian outpost and had sustainable resources

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.400
They could feasibly save the species

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.920
But it would take a long, long time to rebuild civilization as we know it

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.960
And there's your depressing speculation for the day

